{"result": {"response": {"id": "resp_0880790e2e0df4190169bb1633c6f0819191a72e704277f83b", "created": 1773868595, "model": "gpt-5.2-2025-12-11", "object": "chat.completion", "system_fingerprint": null, "choices": [{"finish_reason": "stop", "index": 0, "message": {"content": "**CT SCAN (Computed Tomography / CAT scan)** is a diagnostic medical imaging test that uses multiple x-ray images taken from different angles and computer processing to create detailed cross-sectional internal pictures, which can often be reconstructed into three-dimensional (3-D) images. During a **CT SCAN**, the patient typically lies face up on a motorized table that moves through a large, tunnel-like scanner while images are acquired; the exam is generally described as quick and not painful.\n\nA **CT SCAN** may be performed **with or without contrast**. When used, contrast is commonly given **intravenously and/or orally** to improve image clarity and help distinguish organs, blood vessels, tumors, and lymph nodes.\n\nClinically, **CT SCAN** is used broadly to assess disease presence and extent across many conditions, especially cancer. It may be used when **MRI is unavailable or not recommended**. Examples of oncologic uses include imaging the **head, neck, chest, abdomen, and pelvis** to look for cancer, evaluate tumor extent, detect enlarged lymph nodes, and determine whether disease has spread (for example, CT of the **abdomen and pelvis** to assess spread beyond the bladder, CT of the **chest/abdomen/pelvis** for colon cancer staging, and CT of the **abdomen/pelvis/chest** to evaluate ovarian cancer spread and lymph node enlargement). A **CT SCAN** may also reveal findings incidentally, such as **adrenal tumors** discovered when imaging is performed for another reason, and it can be part of imaging options used to assess the extent of **prostate cancer**.\n\n**CT SCAN** can be used in procedural planning and guidance. It may help **localize targets to guide biopsies**, including lymph node biopsy, and it can be combined with positron emission tomography as **PET/CT**. It is also used during **radiation therapy simulation/planning** to generate the images needed to design a patient-specific radiation treatment plan.\n\nIn follow-up care, **CT SCAN** may be used for surveillance, such as scheduled **CT chest/abdomen/pelvis with contrast** or **CT chest without contrast** performed annually for a defined period (for example, three years) after treatment for recurrence.", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, "provider_specific_fields": {"native_finish_reason": "stop"}}], "usage": {"completion_tokens": 468, "prompt_tokens": 876, "total_tokens": 1344, "completion_tokens_details": {"accepted_prediction_tokens": null, "audio_tokens": null, "reasoning_tokens": 0, "rejected_prediction_tokens": null, "text_tokens": null}, "prompt_tokens_details": {"audio_tokens": null, "cached_tokens": 0, "text_tokens": null, "image_tokens": null}}, "service_tier": null}}}